WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.712
This guided practice combines several data-changing
steps into one small workflow First

00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.262
insert a new patient with a unique MRN Use RETURNING
so you

00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:15.425
can immediately see the row that PostgreSQL stored
Second run a SELECT

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:21.000
using that MRN This is your verification step It
confirms that

00:00:21.025 --> 00:00:25.438
you can retrieve the same row using a stable key
Third update

00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:29.688
the patient's age by one year Again use the MRN in
the

00:00:29.712 --> 00:00:33.850
WHERE clause so the target is precise Add RETURNING
so you can

00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:38.388
inspect the changed row This practice is important
because it reflects a

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.738
more realistic workflow than an isolated SQL command
Data work often follows

00:00:43.762 --> 00:00:48.638
a sequence create or change something then verify it
Use the comments

00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:51.988
in the editor as a guide You do not have to write

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.462
everything from memory Look back at the INSERT and
UPDATE examples if

00:00:56.475 --> 00:01:01.275
needed After the statements run compare the original
inserted age with the

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.938
updated age The identifying MRN and name should
remain the same while

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.475
the age changes Also pay attention to errors If you
accidentally

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:15.600
reuse an MRN that already exists a uniqueness
constraint may reject the

00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:20.412
insert That is a good reminder that the database
schema continues protecting

00:01:20.438 --> 00:01:22.262
the data while you practice DML
